      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0001"
      ],
      "text": "Resistivity depends on temperature — usually in metals",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0002",
      "normalized_math": "R \\uparrow \\qquad T \\uparrow",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0002"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "R increases; T increases",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0003",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0003"
      ],
      "text": "(Superconductors — materials with zero resistance — are generally extremely low temperature. “High temperature” superconductors are those which exhibit R=0 above 77K, the boiling point of nitrogen.)",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0004",
      "level": 1,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0004"
      ],
      "text": "Ohmic Materials (Every material in 1B)",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0005",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0005"
      ],
      "text": "Ohmic materials obey Ohm’s Law:",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0006",
      "normalized_math": "I=\\frac{V}{R}",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0007"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "I equals V divided by R",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0007",
      "normalized_math": "I=\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\right)V",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0006"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "I equals one over R times V",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "simple",
      "detailed_figure_description": null,
      "figure_alternative": "Current-versus-voltage graph with vertical axis I and horizontal axis V. Two straight blue lines pass through the origin; one has a steeper positive slope than the other.",
      "id": "page-4-s0008",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0008"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0009",
      "normalized_math": "R\\uparrow \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\frac{\\Delta I}{\\Delta V}\\downarrow",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0009"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "As R increases, delta I over delta V decreases",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0010",
      "level": 2,
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0010"
      ],
      "text": "Ohm’s Law:",
      "type": "heading"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0011",
      "normalized_math": "V=IR",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0011"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "V equals I R",
      "type": "formula"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0012",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0012"
      ],
      "text": "Example  In the circuit below, is V higher at point A or B? What about I?",
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "complexity": "simple",
      "detailed_figure_description": null,
      "figure_alternative": "Single-loop circuit containing a resistor on the top branch between points A and B and a battery on the bottom branch. An arrow above the resistor shows current I flowing from A toward B. The battery’s positive terminal is on the left and negative terminal on the right.",
      "id": "page-4-s0013",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0013"
      ],
      "type": "figure"
    },
    {
      "id": "page-4-s0014",
      "normalized_math": "V=IR,\\quad \\text{so}\\quad V_A>V_B",
      "page": 4,
      "source_regions": [
        "page-4-r0014"
      ],
      "spoken_math_alternative": "V equals I R, so V sub A is greater than V sub B",
      "type": "formula"
    }
